{
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "text": "IBM's SIC code in edgar.company_profiles contains leading or trailing whitespace, so it does not exactly match any sic_code in company_dns.sic_codes_table.",
      "how_to_test": "SELECT cp.cik, cp.entity_name, cp.sic_code AS raw_sic, TRIM(cp.sic_code) AS trimmed_sic FROM edgar.company_profiles cp WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%' AND cp.sic_code IS NOT NULL AND cp.sic_code <> TRIM(cp.sic_code);\nIf rows are returned, the whitespace hypothesis is supported. Then test the join: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM edgar.company_profiles cp JOIN company_dns.sic_codes_table ct ON TRIM(cp.sic_code) = ct.sic_code WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%';"
    },
    {
      "text": "IBM's SIC code is stored as a numeric value without leading zeros, whereas the reference table expects a zero‑padded 4‑character string (e.g., '0357' vs 357).",
      "how_to_test": "SELECT cp.cik, cp.entity_name, cp.sic_code, LPAD(cp.sic_code::text, 4, '0') AS padded_sic FROM edgar.company_profiles cp WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%' AND cp.sic_code ~ '^\\d+$'; -- ensure it's numeric\nThen test the join with padding: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM edgar.company_profiles cp JOIN company_dns.sic_codes_table ct ON LPAD(cp.sic_code::text, 4, '0') = ct.sic_code WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%';"
    },
    {
      "text": "IBM's SIC code is missing or NULL in edgar.company_profiles, causing the join to drop the row.",
      "how_to_test": "SELECT cik, entity_name, sic_code FROM edgar.company_profiles WHERE entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%' AND sic_code IS NULL;\nIf a row is returned, the missing‑value hypothesis holds. To confirm impact on the join: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM edgar.company_profiles cp LEFT JOIN company_dns.sic_codes_table ct ON cp.sic_code = ct.sic_code WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%' AND ct.sic_code IS NULL;"
    },
    {
      "text": "The company_dns.sic_codes_table lacks coverage for IBM's specific SIC code (e.g., a newer or industry‑specific code) due to a reference‑data gap.",
      "how_to_test": "SELECT DISTINCT cp.sic_code FROM edgar.company_profiles cp WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%' AND cp.sic_code IS NOT NULL EXCEPT SELECT sic_code FROM company_dns.sic_codes_table;\nIf any sic_code values appear in the result, those codes are absent from the reference table, indicating a coverage gap.\nThen verify the join failure: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM edgar.company_profiles cp LEFT JOIN company_dns.sic_codes_table ct ON cp.sic_code = ct.sic_code WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%' AND ct.sic_code IS NULL;"
    },
    {
      "text": "IBM reports multiple SIC codes over time (different lines of business), but edgar.company_profiles stores only one (perhaps an outdated primary code) that does not match the current reference list, while the true activity spans several SICs present in the reference.",
      "how_to_test": "SELECT cp.cik, cp.entity_name, cp.sic_code, COUNT(DISTINCT cp.sic_code) AS sic_count FROM edgar.company_profiles cp WHERE cp.entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%' GROUP BY cp.cik, cp.entity_name, cp.sic_code;\nIf sic_count > 1 across different filings (need to check historical profiles), indicates multiple codes.\nTo test, examine the revenue_facts for IBM to see if different SICs are implied via other company attributes (if available) or check external sources; within EDGAR we can approximate by looking at distinct sic_code values across all company_profile rows for IBM over time (if table includes effective dates). If multiple distinct sic_code values exist, the single‑code hypothesis is plausible.\nFinally, test join using the set of all IBM sic_codes: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (SELECT DISTINCT sic_code FROM edgar.company_profiles WHERE entity_name ILIKE '%international business machines%') cp JOIN company_dns.sic_codes_table ct ON cp.sic_code = ct.sic_code;"
    }
  ]
}
